[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should notifications actually show up in the local phase? → In-app center + catch-up (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that's the honest one, and it needs no permissions.

  Two things left before I can present a design: scope of the first build, and the stack. Scope first.

  Given the decomposition, the multi-user backend (piece B) is clearly its own project later. The question is whether
  pieces [1mA + C[0m (tasks core + event log) ship and get reviewed before [1mD + E[0m (subscriptions + notification center), or
  whether all four are one deliverable.

  For an app this size — one page, localStorage, no server — A+C+D+E is small enough to be a single coherent spec, and
  splitting it means you don't see a notification until the second cycle. The argument for splitting is that you'd get a
  working task app you can actually use sooner, and the notification design could adapt to what you learn using it.
